Panel B — Figure A1.40: Stroboscopic parabolic trajectory of a bouncing ball
Draw a horizontal ground line at bottom. Show 12 identical dark grey ball images at equal horizontal spacing forming a parabolic arc. First 5 balls moving upward and right with decreasing vertical spacing, 1 ball at the peak, 4 balls moving downward and right with increasing vertical spacing, then 3 balls after a bounce with smaller parabola. Label "Δt = constant" between consecutive balls. Add annotated arrows: "Horizontal component constant → equal horizontal separation", "Decreasing vertical separation (deceleration)" on upward segment, "Increasing vertical separation (acceleration)" on downward segment.

Panel C — Figure A1.41: Dropped vs projected horizontally comparison
Draw two side-by-side panels. Left panel: A ball dropped vertically from rest from height h, shown at 4 time instants (t₀, t₁, t₂, t₃) with increasing vertical spacing. Right panel: A ball projected horizontally from the same height, shown at the same time instants at the same vertical levels (connected by dashed horizontal lines). The trajectory is a dashed parabolic arc. Label both panels and add a callout: "Both objects fall equal vertical distances in the same time. Horizontal motion does not affect vertical acceleration."

Panel D — Figure A1.42: Experimental setup for range vs height
Draw a table (light grey rectangle) at the top. On the table, show a small ramp or spring-launcher with a steel ball (black circle) at the edge. A dashed red-orange trajectory line curves from the table edge down to the ground (bottom horizontal line). Label height h with a double-headed arrow from tabletop to ground. Label range x with a double-headed arrow along the ground from the edge to the impact point. Add a callout box: x = v_H · t and t = √(2h/g) → x ∝ √h.

