<!-- SECTION A — HEADER -->
<div class="lc-badge"><span>4</span> Lesson 4 of 12 · A1 Kinematics</div>
<div class="lc-title">Projectile Motion</div>
<div class="lc-sub">How do we predict the path of a thrown ball, a fired bullet, or any object moving under gravity alone? This lesson builds on vertical motion to handle 2D trajectories.</div>

<!-- SECTION B — HOOK & DIAGNOSTIC -->
<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>🎯 Hook – The bullet and the dropped ball</h3>
  <p>Imagine firing a bullet horizontally from a gun at the same instant as you drop another bullet from the same height. Which hits the ground first? Surprisingly — <strong>they both land at exactly the same time</strong>. The horizontal motion does <em>not</em> affect the vertical fall. This is the core insight of projectile motion: horizontal and vertical components are completely independent.</p>
  <div class="lc-img">
    <img src="https://i.ibb.co/sdBX3DZ7/image.png" alt="Comparison of a dropped ball and a horizontally projected ball falling equal vertical distances in the same time">
    <div class="lc-cap">Fig 1 — A dropped ball (left) and a horizontally projected ball (right) fall equal vertical distances in the same time. Horizontal motion does not affect vertical acceleration.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="lc-info">💡 <strong>Diagnostic question (think – then click)</strong> — A stone is thrown horizontally from a cliff. If you ignore air resistance, what happens to its horizontal speed as it falls? <em>Answer: It stays constant — no horizontal force means no horizontal acceleration.</em></div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION C — CORE KNOWLEDGE -->
<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>📐 Resolving velocity into components</h3>
  <p>A projectile's velocity at any instant can be split into <strong>horizontal</strong> and <strong>vertical</strong> components. These components are <strong>independent</strong> — they can be analysed separately.</p>
  <div class="lc-formula">
    <span class="lc-formula-label">Components of velocity</span>
    $$v_{\mathrm{v}} = v\sin\theta \qquad v_{\mathrm{H}} = v\cos\theta$$
  </div>
  <p>Here \(v\) is the magnitude of the velocity, and \(\theta\) is the angle it makes with the horizontal. The horizontal component remains <strong>constant</strong> (if air resistance is negligible), while the vertical component changes at \(g = 9.8\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}\).</p>
  <div class="lc-img">
    <img src="https://i.ibb.co/tpLSv0Gb/image.png" alt="Components of a projectile's velocity vector with tennis player example">
    <div class="lc-cap">Fig 2 — Resolving velocity into horizontal and vertical components. The tennis player example shows a ball hit at 6° below horizontal.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="lc-info">🔑 Key idea: The horizontal and vertical motions are <strong>independent</strong>. The vertical acceleration is always \(g\) downward, regardless of the horizontal motion.</div>
</div>

<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>⏱️ Time of flight and range</h3>
  <p>The <strong>time of flight</strong> is determined entirely by the <strong>vertical</strong> motion. For a projectile launched horizontally from height \(h\):</p>
  <div class="lc-formula">
    <span class="lc-formula-label">Time to fall from height h</span>
    $$h = \frac{1}{2}gt^2 \quad \Rightarrow \quad t = \sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}}$$
  </div>
  <p>The <strong>range</strong> (horizontal distance) is then:</p>
  <div class="lc-formula">
    $$x = v_{\mathrm{H}} \cdot t = v_{\mathrm{H}} \sqrt{\frac{2h}{g}}$$
  </div>
  <p>So the range is proportional to the square root of the height: \(x \propto \sqrt{h}\). This is a key result you will be expected to graph and linearise.</p>
  <div class="lc-img">
    <img src="https://i.ibb.co/VYM0MzyT/image.png" alt="Experimental setup for measuring range x at different heights h">
    <div class="lc-cap">Fig 3 — Experimental setup for measuring range \(x\) for different drop heights \(h\).</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>📈 Graphs every examiner uses</h3>
  <table class="lc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Graph type</th><th>Axes (X, Y)</th><th>Shape</th><th>What to read</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Range vs height</td><td>\(x\) (m), \(h\) (m)</td><td>Parabolic / concave down (\(x \propto \sqrt{h}\))</td><td>\(x\) increases with \(h\) but with diminishing returns</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Linearised range</td><td>\(x\) (m), \(\sqrt{h}\) (\(\sqrt{\mathrm{m}}\))</td><td>Straight line through origin</td><td>Gradient \(= v_{\mathrm{H}}\sqrt{2/g}\)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Vertical velocity vs time</td><td>\(t\) (s), \(v_{\mathrm{v}}\) (m/s)</td><td>Straight line with negative slope \(-g\)</td><td>Slope \(= -9.8\,\) m/s²</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Horizontal velocity vs time</td><td>\(t\) (s), \(v_{\mathrm{H}}\) (m/s)</td><td>Horizontal line (constant)</td><td>\(v_{\mathrm{H}}\) = constant</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="lc-img-row">
    <div class="lc-img"><img src="https://i.ibb.co/H9J08Jr/image.png" alt="Range x vs height h direct plot"><div class="lc-cap">Fig 4a — Direct plot: \(x\) vs \(h\) (concave down, \(x \propto \sqrt{h}\)).</div></div>
    <div class="lc-img"><img src="https://i.ibb.co/4nBD1TsP/image.png" alt="Linearised range x vs sqrt h"><div class="lc-cap">Fig 4b — Linearised plot: \(x\) vs \(\sqrt{h}\) (straight line through origin).</div></div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION E — WORKED EXAMPLE (A1.10: Horizontal bullet) -->
<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>✍️ Worked example – Bullet fired horizontally</h3>
  <div class="lc-step"><div class="lc-step-num">1</div><div class="lc-step-body"><strong>Identify:</strong> A bullet is fired horizontally from height \(h = 22.0\,\mathrm{m}\) with speed \(v_{\mathrm{H}} = 524\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\). We need the horizontal range. Air resistance is negligible. The time of flight comes from vertical motion.</div></div>
  <div class="lc-step"><div class="lc-step-num">2</div><div class="lc-step-body"><strong>Data:</strong> \(h = 22.0\,\mathrm{m}\), \(v_{\mathrm{H}} = 524\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}\), \(g = 9.81\,\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}\).</div></div>
  <div class="lc-step"><div class="lc-step-num">3</div><div class="lc-step-body"><strong>Equation:</strong> From \(h = \frac{1}{2}gt^2\): \(t = \sqrt{2h/g}\). Then \(x = v_{\mathrm{H}} t\).</div></div>
  <div class="lc-step"><div class="lc-step-num">4</div><div class="lc-step-body"><strong>Answer:</strong> \(t = \sqrt{2(22.0)/9.81} = 2.12\,\mathrm{s}\). \(x = 524 \times 2.12 = 1.11 \times 10^3\,\mathrm{m}\) (1.11 km). The bullet lands 1.11 km away.</div></div>
  <div class="lc-img">
    <img src="https://i.ibb.co/VYM0MzyT/image.png" alt="Bullet fired horizontally from height 22.0 m showing range 1.11 km">
    <div class="lc-cap">Fig 5 — Setup for the bullet example: range \(x = 1.11\) km from height 22.0 m.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION F — REAL-WORLD + HL EXTENSION -->
<div class="lc-two-col">
  <div class="lc-card">
    <h3>🎯 Real-world: Sports projectile analysis</h3>
    <p>In sports like tennis, golf, and basketball, players intuitively adjust launch angle and speed. The optimal angle for maximum range on level ground is \(45^\circ\). However, air resistance (drag) reduces range, so real athletes often launch lower or higher depending on the sport.</p>
    <div class="lc-success">✓ Examiner tip: In IB exam questions, <strong>always state assumptions</strong> about air resistance. If the question says "negligible air resistance", you must use the constant horizontal velocity model.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="lc-card">
    <h3>🚀 HL Extension – Projectile with drag</h3>
    <p>In reality, air resistance adds a force opposite to velocity. This makes the trajectory <strong>non‑parabolic</strong> — the range is shorter, the time of flight is different, and the descent is steeper than the ascent.</p>
    <p><strong>Challenge:</strong> If air resistance is included, does the time of flight increase, decrease, or stay the same for a projectile launched upwards? (Answer: It increases, because drag slows the upward motion and reduces the peak height, but the total fall time is longer due to the reduced downward acceleration.)</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION G — MISCONCEPTIONS / TRAP CORNER -->
<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>⚠️ Trap corner – common mistakes</h3>
  <table class="lc-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Misconception</th><th>Correct view</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>A projectile's horizontal velocity changes as it falls.</td><td>Horizontal velocity is <strong>constant</strong> (if no air resistance). Only the vertical component changes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones.</td><td>In the absence of air resistance, all objects fall with the same acceleration \(g\), regardless of mass.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The time of flight depends on the horizontal speed.</td><td>The time of flight is determined <strong>only</strong> by vertical motion. A faster horizontal speed does <em>not</em> keep the projectile in the air longer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td>The trajectory of a projectile is always a full parabola.</td><td>Only the path <em>between launch and landing</em> is parabolic. If the ground is not level, the visible arc may be only part of a parabola.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <div class="lc-warn">⚠️ Most common slip: <strong>Using the initial velocity as the horizontal component</strong>. Always resolve the velocity into components first using \(v_{\mathrm{H}} = v\cos\theta\) and \(v_{\mathrm{v}} = v\sin\theta\).</div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION H — KEY POINTS PILLS -->
<div class="lc-kp">
  <span>\(v_{\mathrm{H}} = v\cos\theta\)</span>
  <span>\(v_{\mathrm{v}} = v\sin\theta\)</span>
  <span>\(t = \sqrt{2h/g}\)</span>
  <span>\(x = v_{\mathrm{H}}t\)</span>
  <span>\(x \propto \sqrt{h}\)</span>
  <span>\(a_{\mathrm{H}} = 0\)</span>
</div>

<!-- SECTION I — MCQ PRACTICE -->
<div class="lc-section">Practice check — multiple choice</div>

