<div class="lc-title">Gravitational Field Strength</div>
<div class="lc-sub">Why does a 1 kg rock weigh 9.8 N on Earth but only 1.6 N on the Moon? Learn how to predict gravity anywhere in the universe.</div>

<!-- SECTION A: HOOK & DIAGNOSTIC -->
<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>🎯 Hook – Moon vs Earth gravity</h3>
  <p>Watch an astronaut on the Moon: they can jump six times higher than on Earth with the same effort. The <strong>gravitational field strength</strong> <i>g</i> on the Moon is only about 1.6 N/kg, compared to 9.8 N/kg on Earth. This means every kilogram of mass experiences a much smaller pull.</p>
  <div class="lc-img">
    <img src="https://i.postimg.cc/wvPMyZHR/image.png" alt="Astronaut jumping on Moon vs struggling on Earth">
    <div class="lc-cap">Fig 1 — Same astronaut, different worlds: field strength determines weight.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="lc-info">💡 <strong>Diagnostic question (think – then click)</strong> — If you travel to a planet with twice Earth's mass and the same radius, your weight would be: <em>twice as much (because g = GM/R²)</em>.</div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION B: CORE KNOWLEDGE (two cards) -->
<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>📐 Definition of gravitational field strength</h3>
  <p><strong>Gravitational field strength</strong> <i>g</i> at a point is the force per unit mass experienced by a small point mass placed at that point. It is a vector quantity – direction is towards the mass creating the field.</p>
  <div class="lc-formula">
    <span class="lc-formula-label">Definition</span>
    $$g = \frac{F}{m}$$
  </div>
  <p>Units: N kg⁻¹ (equivalent to m s⁻²). The test mass must be small so it does not disturb the original field.</p>
  <div class="lc-img">
    <img src="https://i.postimg.cc/W3R16KtB/image.png" alt="Test mass m near large mass M with force arrow">
    <div class="lc-cap">Fig 2 — A small test mass m feels force F = mg; g depends only on M and r.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="lc-card">
  <h3>📐 Field from a large mass – inverse‑square law</h3>
  <p>Combine Newton's law of gravitation \(F = \frac{GMm}{r^2}\) with \(g = F/m\) to get the field due to a point mass or spherical mass M at distance r from its centre:</p>
  <div class="lc-formula">
    <span class="lc-formula-label">Gravitational field strength from a mass M</span>
    $$g = \frac{GM}{r^2}$$
  </div>
  <p>Notice that the small mass m cancels – so all objects fall with the same acceleration in a given field. The field follows an <strong>inverse‑square law</strong>: doubling r reduces g to one‑quarter.</p>
